export const c12Phy8HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FFE082; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit VIII: Atoms and Nuclei</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Bohr Radius (a<sub>0</sub> / r<sub>1</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Radius of the innermost stable ground state orbit (n = 1) of hydrogen atom: a<sub>0</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> h<sup>2</sup> / (&pi; m e<sup>2</sup>) &approx; 0.529 &Aring; = 0.0529 nm [Meter m, [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Rydberg Constant (R):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Fundamental spectroscopic constant appearing in Rydberg formula: R = m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c h<sup>3</sup>) &approx; 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup> [m<sup>-1</sup>, [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>-1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Nuclear Radius (R):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Effective radius of an atomic nucleus of mass number A: R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup>, where R<sub>0</sub> &approx; 1.2 fm = 1.2 &times; 10<sup>-15</sup> m [Femtometer fm / m, [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Atomic Mass Unit (1 u / 1 amu):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Standard unit of mass defined as exactly 1/12th the mass of one unbound neutral Carbon-12 atom: 1 u = 1.6605 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup> kg &approx; 931.5 MeV/c<sup>2</sup>.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Mass Defect (&Delta;m):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Difference between the total mass of individual constituent nucleons and the actual resting mass of the bound nucleus: &Delta;m = [Z m<sub>p</sub> + (A - Z) m<sub>n</sub>] - M<sub>nucleus</sub> [kg / u].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FDC830; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Binding Energy per Nucleon (E<sub>bn</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Average energy required to extract one nucleon from the nucleus: E<sub>bn</sub> = E<sub>b</sub> / A = (&Delta;m &times; 931.5) / A [MeV/nucleon, [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">PART I: Atoms (Atomic Structure &amp; Spectra)</h2>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 25px;\">1. &alpha;-Particle Scattering Experiment &amp; Rutherford's Model of Atom</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Geiger-Marsden &alpha;-Particle Scattering Experiment (1911)</h3>\n<p>Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden, under the guidance of Ernest Rutherford, directed a collimated beam of 5.5 MeV &alpha;-particles (helium nuclei <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He<sup>2+</sup>) from a radioactive Bismuth-214 source onto an ultra-thin gold foil (~2.1 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m thick, about 400 atoms thick). The scattered &alpha;-particles were detected by flashes of light (scintillations) produced on a rotatable zinc sulfide (ZnS) fluorescent screen viewed through a microscope.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 4px solid #76FF03; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #76FF03; font-size: 15.5px;\">Key Experimental Observations:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 16px; padding: 0; line-height: 1.7; color: #FFFFFF;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Over 99.86%</b> of incident &alpha;-particles passed straight through the gold foil with virtually zero deflection (&theta; &approx; 0&deg;).</li>\n      <li>2. Only about <b>0.14%</b> of &alpha;-particles were scattered by angles greater than 1&deg;.</li>\n      <li>3. Extremely rare &alpha;-particles (about <b>1 in 8,000</b>) suffered large-angle deflections (&theta; &gt; 90&deg;).</li>\n      <li>4. Exceptionally rare particles (about 1 in 10<sup>6</sup>) bounced back with &theta; &approx; 180&deg;!</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 1: Alpha-Particle Scattering Experiment\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.1:</b> Alpha-Particle Scattering Experiment &ndash; Alpha-particles from a lead block collimator directed at an ultra-thin gold foil; most pass straight through while rare particles scatter at large angles on the ZnS screen.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #FFE082; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFE082; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Rutherford's Astonishment: Firing a Cannonball at Tissue Paper</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Rutherford famously described the 180&deg; backward bounce of &alpha;-particles: <i>'It was quite the most incredible event that has ever happened to me in my life. It was almost as incredible as if you fired a 15-inch artillery shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you!'</i> This proved that positive charge is not spread out thinly (Thomson's plum pudding model), but is concentrated in a tiny, dense, massive core called the <b>Nucleus</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Distance of Closest Approach (r_0) &amp; Estimate of Nuclear Size</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Head-on Collision &amp; Conservation of Energy):</b><br>\n    For a head-on collision (&theta; = 180&deg;, impact parameter b = 0), an &alpha;-particle of mass m, charge q<sub>1</sub> = +2e, and initial kinetic energy K = 1/2 m v<sup>2</sup> approaches the gold nucleus (charge q<sub>2</sub> = +Ze) along the line of centers.<br>\n    As it approaches, kinetic energy is continuously converted into electrostatic potential energy until it momentarily stops at distance r<sub>0</sub>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Equating Initial Kinetic Energy to Electrostatic Potential Energy at r<sub>0</sub>):</b><br>\n    K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(2e)(Ze)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 Z e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>0</sub></span></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Solving for Distance of Closest Approach r<sub>0</sub>):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>r<sub>0</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 Z e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> K</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 Z e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (m v<sup>2</sup>)</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n    For 5.5 MeV &alpha;-particles on gold (Z = 79): r<sub>0</sub> &approx; 4.13 &times; 10<sup>-14</sup> m = <b>41.3 fm</b>. Since the nucleus must be smaller than r<sub>0</sub>, actual nuclear radius is &le; 10<sup>-14</sup> m.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 2: Rutherford's Planetary Model of Atom\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.2:</b> Rutherford's Model of Atom &ndash; Tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus at the center with planetary electrons orbiting around it; most of the atom is vast empty space.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Why Did Rutherford's Planetary Atomic Model Fail Classically?</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n    1. <b>Orbital Instability:</b> According to Maxwell's classical electrodynamics, an orbiting electron in accelerated circular motion must continuously radiate electromagnetic energy. Losing energy, the electron's orbital radius would spiral inward, crashing into the nucleus in less than <b>10<sup>-8</sup> seconds</b>! Thus, classical atoms cannot be stable.<br>\n    2. <b>Failure to Explain Discrete Line Spectra:</b> A continuously spiraling electron should radiate a continuous frequency spectrum of light. In reality, atoms emit sharp, discrete spectral lines!\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 35px;\">2. Bohr's Quantum Model of Hydrogen Atom</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Bohr's Three Fundamental Postulates (1913)</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li>1. <b>Postulate of Stationary Non-Radiating Orbits:</b> An electron in an atom revolves around the nucleus only in certain discrete, non-radiating circular orbits called <b>stationary states</b>. While in these orbits, the electron does not emit electromagnetic radiation.</li>\n    <li>2. <b>Postulate of Angular Momentum Quantization:</b> The orbital angular momentum (L) of an electron revolving in a stable orbit is an integral multiple of h/(2&pi;):<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>L = m v r = n <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &pi;</span></span> = n &hbar;</b></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; (where n = 1, 2, 3, ... is Principal Quantum Number).</li>\n    <li>3. <b>Postulate of Quantum Transition Radiation:</b> An electron emits or absorbs radiation of a discrete frequency &nu; only when jumping from one stationary orbit of energy E<sub>2</sub> to another of energy E<sub>1</sub>:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>h &nu; = E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub> &nbsp;&hArr;&nbsp; &nu; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">h</span></span></b></span>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 3: Bohr Model of Hydrogen Atom\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.3:</b> Bohr Model of Hydrogen Atom &ndash; Electrons revolve in stable, quantized, non-radiating orbits with definite energy states (n = 1, 2, 3...).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 4: Expression for Radius of n-th Orbit\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.4:</b> Radius of n-th Possible Orbit &ndash; r<sub>n</sub> = n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> m k e<sup>2</sup>) = n<sup>2</sup> a<sub>0</sub> (where ground state Bohr radius a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 &Aring;).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 5: Orbital Velocity of Electron in n-th Orbit\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.5:</b> Orbital Velocity in n-th Orbit &ndash; v<sub>n</sub> = k e<sup>2</sup> / (n &hbar;) = v<sub>1</sub> / n (orbital speed decreases inversely with quantum number n).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Derivation of Radius (r_n), Velocity (v_n), and Total Energy (E_n) of Electron in n-th Bohr Orbit</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Electrostatic Centripetal Equilibrium for Hydrogen Z = 1):</b><br>\n    Coulomb attraction provides the necessary centripetal force:<br>\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>m v<sup>2</sup> r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b></span>. &nbsp;&nbsp; ...(1)<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Bohr's Quantization Condition):</b><br>\n    m v r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &pi;</span></span> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &pi; m r</span></span></b></span>. &nbsp;&nbsp; ...(2)<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Radius of n-th Orbit r<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n    Substituting (2) into (1): m [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi;<sup>2</sup> m<sup>2</sup> r<sup>2</sup></span></span>] r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> &rArr;<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>r<sub>n</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi; m e<sup>2</sup></span></span> = n<sup>2</sup> a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 n<sup>2</sup> &Aring; = 0.0529 n<sup>2</sup> nm</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Orbital Velocity v<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n    Substituting r<sub>n</sub> into (2):<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>v<sub>n</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> n h</span></span> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">137</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n</span></span> &approx; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2.18 &times; 10<sup>6</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n</span></span> m/s</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 5 (Kinetic, Potential &amp; Total Energy E<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n    • Kinetic Energy: K = 1/2 m v<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>n</sub></span></span> = + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>\n    • Potential Energy: U = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>n</sub></span></span> = - 2 K = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>\n    • Total Energy: E<sub>n</sub> = K + U = - K = 1/2 U:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 18px;\"><b>E<sub>n</sub> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">13.6</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sup>2</sup></span></span> eV</b></span>.<br>\n    <i>Negative Sign:</i> The negative total energy signifies that the electron is stably bound to the nucleus.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 6: Total Energy of Electron in n-th Orbit\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.6:</b> Energy of Electron in n-th Orbit &ndash; E<sub>n</sub> = - m e<sup>4</sup> k<sup>2</sup> / (2 &hbar;<sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup>) = - E<sub>1</sub> / n<sup>2</sup> = - 13.6 / n<sup>2</sup> eV.</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 35px;\">3. Hydrogen Emission Line Spectra &amp; Spectral Series</h2>\n<p>When an electron jumps from a higher energy orbit n<sub>2</sub> to a lower orbit n<sub>1</sub> (n<sub>2</sub> &gt; n<sub>1</sub>), the emitted photon wavelength is given by the <b>Rydberg Formula</b>:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 4px solid #76FF03; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&lambda;</span></span> = &nu;&#772; = R [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> ]</b></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; (where R = 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>).\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 7: Electron Quantum Transition &amp; Spectral Line Emission\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.7:</b> Electron Transition &amp; Spectral Lines &ndash; When an electron transitions from a higher orbit to a lower orbit, energy is emitted as a discrete photon (h&nu; = E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub>), producing characteristic spectral lines.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 10px;\">🌈 The 5 Characteristic Spectral Series of Hydrogen Atom:</b>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #E040FB; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #E040FB;\">1. Lyman Series:</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n<sub>1</sub> = 1, n<sub>2</sub> = 2, 3, 4, ... &infin;<br><b>Spectral Region:</b> Ultraviolet (UV)<br>Shortest &lambda; = 91.2 nm, Longest &lambda; = 121.6 nm.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00E5FF;\">2. Balmer Series:</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n<sub>1</sub> = 2, n<sub>2</sub> = 3, 4, 5, ... &infin;<br><b>Spectral Region:</b> Visible Light (H<sub>&alpha;</sub> = 656.3 nm red, H<sub>&beta;</sub> = 486.1 nm blue-green).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #76FF03; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">3. Paschen Series:</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n<sub>1</sub> = 3, n<sub>2</sub> = 4, 5, 6, ... &infin;<br><b>Spectral Region:</b> Near Infrared (IR).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF9100; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF9100;\">4. Brackett Series:</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n<sub>1</sub> = 4, n<sub>2</sub> = 5, 6, 7, ... &infin;<br><b>Spectral Region:</b> Infrared (IR).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">5. Pfund Series:</b><br>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n<sub>1</sub> = 5, n<sub>2</sub> = 6, 7, 8, ... &infin;<br><b>Spectral Region:</b> Far Infrared (Far IR).</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">PART II: Nuclei (Nuclear Structure, Forces &amp; Energy)</h2>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 25px;\">4. Composition and Size of Nucleus &amp; Strong Nuclear Force</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Nuclear Composition &amp; Size</h3>\n<p>An atomic nucleus <sup>A</sup><sub>Z</sub>X consists of Z protons and N = (A - Z) neutrons, collectively termed <b>nucleons</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 360px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 8: Structure and Composition of Atomic Nucleus\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 8.8:</b> Atomic Nucleus &ndash; Packed with positively charged Protons (+) and uncharged Neutrons (0); extremely compact in size but containing over 99.9% of the total atomic mass.</p>\n  </div>\n<ul>\n    <li>• <b>Nuclear Radius:</b> <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup></b></span> (where R<sub>0</sub> &approx; 1.2 &times; 10<sup>-15</sup> m = 1.2 fm).</li>\n    <li>• <b>Nuclear Volume:</b> V = 4/3 &pi; R<sup>3</sup> = 4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A &prop; A (Volume is directly proportional to mass number A).</li>\n    <li>• <b>Invariable Nuclear Density (&rho;):</b> Mass of nucleus M &approx; A m<sub>n</sub>. Volume V &approx; 4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A.<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>&rho; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Mass</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Volume</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A m<sub>n</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3 m<sub>n</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup></span></span> &approx; 2.3 &times; 10<sup>17</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup></b></span>.<br>\n    <i>Monumental Fact:</i> Nuclear density is <b>completely independent of mass number A</b> &ndash; all nuclei from Hydrogen to Uranium have the exact same colossal density!</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #FFE082; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFE082; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: How Incomprehensibly Dense is Nuclear Matter?</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A single teaspoon (1 cm<sup>3</sup>) of pure nuclear matter weighs approximately <b>230 million metric tons</b> &ndash; equivalent to packing the combined weight of 50 million fully-loaded pickup trucks into a sugar cube!</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Fundamental Properties of Strong Nuclear Force</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Strongest Force in Nature:</b> About <b>100 times stronger</b> than electromagnetic electrostatic repulsion and 10<sup>38</sup> times stronger than gravitation.</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Extremely Short Range:</b> Acts only over nuclear distances (~1 to 2 fm). Beyond 2 fm, the force vanishes to zero.</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Charge Independence:</b> Operates with equal strength between proton-proton (p-p), proton-neutron (p-n), and neutron-neutron (n-n): <b>F<sub>pp</sub> = F<sub>pn</sub> = F<sub>nn</sub></b>.</li>\n      <li>4. <b>Saturation Property:</b> A nucleon interacts only with its immediate neighboring nucleons, not with all nucleons in the nucleus.</li>\n      <li>5. <b>Non-Central &amp; Spin-Dependent:</b> Depends on the mutual spin orientation of nucleons and is not directed strictly along the line joining centers.</li>\n      <li>6. <b>Repulsive Core:</b> Becomes strongly repulsive at extremely short separations (r &lt; 0.8 fm), preventing the nucleus from collapsing into an infinite singularity.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 35px;\">5. Mass-Energy Equivalence, Mass Defect &amp; Binding Energy per Nucleon</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Mass Defect (&Delta;m) and Binding Energy per Nucleon (E_bn)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Einstein's Mass-Energy Equivalence):</b><br>\n    Mass and energy are interconvertible: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>E = &Delta;m &times; c<sup>2</sup></b></span>.<br>\n    Energy equivalent of 1 u: E = (1.6605 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup> kg)(3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s)<sup>2</sup> = 1.494 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> J = <b>931.5 MeV</b>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Mass Defect &Delta;m):</b><br>\n    For a nucleus <sup>A</sup><sub>Z</sub>X containing Z protons and (A - Z) neutrons with measured nuclear mass M:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>&Delta;m = [ Z m<sub>p</sub> + (A - Z) m<sub>n</sub> ] - M<sub>nucleus</sub></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Total Nuclear Binding Energy E<sub>b</sub>):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>E<sub>b</sub> = &Delta;m &times; c<sup>2</sup> = &Delta;m(in u) &times; 931.5 MeV</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Binding Energy per Nucleon E<sub>bn</sub>):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>E<sub>bn</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>b</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;m &times; 931.5</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> MeV / nucleon</b></span>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Binding Energy per Nucleon Curve &amp; Its Physical Significance</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Key Features of the E<sub>bn</sub> vs Mass Number (A) Curve:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFFFFF;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Peak of Maximum Stability:</b> The curve reaches its absolute maximum at <b>Iron-56 (<sup>56</sup><sub>26</sub>Fe) with E<sub>bn</sub> = 8.75 MeV/nucleon</b>, making Iron the most tightly bound and stable nucleus in the universe.</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Broad Flat Plateau:</b> For intermediate nuclei (30 &le; A &le; 170), E<sub>bn</sub> is nearly constant around <b>~8.5 MeV/nucleon</b> due to nuclear force saturation.</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Drop for Heavy Nuclei (A &gt; 170):</b> E<sub>bn</sub> drops steadily to ~7.6 MeV/nucleon for Uranium (<sup>235</sup>U) due to increasing Coulomb electrostatic repulsion among many protons.</li>\n      <li>4. <b>Low Values for Light Nuclei (A &lt; 30):</b> Very light nuclei (<sup>1</sup>H, <sup>2</sup>H, <sup>3</sup>H) have low binding energy per nucleon (e.g. Deuteron ~1.1 MeV/nucleon).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 35px;\">6. Nuclear Fission &amp; Nuclear Fusion (Release of Nuclear Energy)</h2>\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1.2px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💥 1. Nuclear Fission (Heavy Nucleus Splitting):</b>\n      <p style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">A heavy unstable nucleus (A &gt; 230) captures a thermal neutron and splits into two lighter, more stable intermediate nuclei with higher E<sub>bn</sub>, releasing huge energy (~200 MeV per fission):</p>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.4); padding: 8px 10px; border-radius: 6px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; font-size: 13.5px;\">\n        <sup>235</sup><sub>92</sub>U + <sup>1</sup><sub>0</sub>n &rarr; <sup>144</sup><sub>56</sub>Ba + <sup>89</sup><sub>36</sub>Kr + 3 <sup>1</sup><sub>0</sub>n + Q (200 MeV)\n      </div>\n      <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13px; margin: 6px 0 0 0;\">Applied in: Nuclear Power Reactors &amp; Atomic Fission Weapons.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1.2px solid #FFD54F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FFD54F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">☀️ 2. Nuclear Fusion (Light Nuclei Combining):</b>\n      <p style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Two extremely light nuclei (A &le; 10) fuse together at thermonuclear temperatures (T ~ 10<sup>7</sup> K) to form a heavier, more tightly bound nucleus, releasing colossal energy:</p>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.4); padding: 8px 10px; border-radius: 6px; font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; font-size: 13.5px;\">\n        4 <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H &rarr; <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He + 2 e<sup>+</sup> + 2 &nu; + 26.7 MeV\n      </div>\n      <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13px; margin: 6px 0 0 0;\">Source of Solar Stellar Energy &amp; Hydrogen (Thermonuclear) Bombs.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 35px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet &bull; Unit VIII: Atoms &amp; Nuclei</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Closest Approach:</b> r<sub>0</sub> = 2 Z e<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> K)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Bohr Radius:</b> r<sub>n</sub> = n<sup>2</sup> a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 n<sup>2</sup> &Aring;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Bohr Velocity:</b> v<sub>n</sub> = (2.18 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) / n m/s</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Bohr Energy:</b> E<sub>n</sub> = -13.6 / n<sup>2</sup> eV &nbsp;|&nbsp; K = -E, U = 2E</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Rydberg Formula:</b> 1/&lambda; = R [1/n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> - 1/n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>]</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Nuclear Radius:</b> R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup> (R<sub>0</sub> = 1.2 fm)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Nuclear Density:</b> &rho; = 2.3 &times; 10<sup>17</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup> = const</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Mass Energy:</b> 1 u = 931.5 MeV &nbsp;|&nbsp; E = &Delta;m c<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Mass Defect:</b> &Delta;m = [Z m<sub>p</sub> + (A-Z)m<sub>n</sub>] - M</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Binding Energy / Nucleon:</b> E<sub>bn</sub> = E<sub>b</sub> / A</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Peak Stability:</b> <sup>56</sup>Fe (E<sub>bn</sub> = 8.75 MeV/nucleon)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Fission Energy:</b> ~200 MeV per <sup>235</sup>U fission</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

export const c12Phy8HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 4px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">🎯 Complete 100 Board Solutions &bull; Unit VIII: Atoms &amp; Nuclei</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FFE082; margin: 0; font-size: 14px; text-align: center !important;\">Section A (Q1–Q40: 1 Mark VSA) &bull; Section B (Q41–Q80: 2 &amp; 3 Marks SA) &bull; Section C (Q81–Q100: 5 Marks LA)</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">SECTION A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark Each &bull; Q1 to Q40)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q1: What is the charge and mass of an &alpha;-particle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An &alpha;-particle is a doubly ionized helium nucleus (<sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He<sup>2+</sup>) with charge <b>q = +2e = +3.2 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C</b> and mass <b>m &approx; 4 u = 6.64 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup> kg</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q2: Why was a thin gold foil used in the Geiger-Marsden experiment?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Gold is extremely malleable and can be hammered into an ultra-thin sheet (~400 atoms thick), ensuring an &alpha;-particle undergoes only a <b>single scattering collision</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q3: Define impact parameter in &alpha;-particle scattering.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Impact parameter (b) is the perpendicular distance of the initial velocity vector of the &alpha;-particle from the center of the target nucleus.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q4: What is the value of the impact parameter for a head-on collision (&theta; = 180&deg;)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For a head-on collision, the impact parameter is <b>b = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q5: Define distance of closest approach.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The minimum distance (r<sub>0</sub>) up to which an incident &alpha;-particle traveling directly toward the target nucleus can approach before momentarily coming to rest and reversing its path.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q6: State Bohr's quantization condition for angular momentum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The orbital angular momentum of an electron in a stationary orbit is an integral multiple of h/(2&pi;): <b>L = m v r = n h / (2&pi;) = n &hbar;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q7: What is the radius of the first orbit of hydrogen atom (Bohr radius)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The Bohr radius is <b>a<sub>0</sub> = r<sub>1</sub> = 0.529 &Aring; = 0.0529 nm = 5.29 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q8: How does the radius of the n-th Bohr orbit depend on principal quantum number n?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The radius is directly proportional to the square of principal quantum number: <b>r<sub>n</sub> &prop; n<sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q9: Write the expression for the total energy of an electron in the n-th Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Total energy is: <b>E<sub>n</sub> = - 13.6 / n<sup>2</sup> eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q10: What is the ground state energy of hydrogen atom?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For n = 1, ground state energy is <b>E<sub>1</sub> = - 13.6 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q11: Define ionization energy of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Ionization energy is the minimum energy required to remove an electron completely from the ground state to infinity (n = &infin;): <b>E<sub>ion</sub> = + 13.6 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q12: What is the ratio of kinetic energy to potential energy of an electron in a Bohr orbit?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Ratio of kinetic energy to potential energy is: <b>K / U = (+K) / (-2K) = - 1 / 2 = - 0.5</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q13: Name the spectral series of hydrogen that lies in the visible region.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The <b>Balmer series</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q14: Name the spectral series of hydrogen that lies in the ultraviolet region.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The <b>Lyman series</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q15: Which spectral series of hydrogen lies in the infrared region?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Paschen, Brackett, and Pfund series</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q16: State the value and SI unit of the Rydberg constant (R).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Rydberg constant: <b>R = 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q17: What is the wavelength of the H_&alpha; line in the Balmer series?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The H<sub>&alpha;</sub> line (transition from n = 3 to n = 2) has wavelength <b>&lambda; = 656.3 nm (6563 &Aring;)</b> (red light).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q18: What is the ratio of nuclear radius to atomic radius?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear radius (~10<sup>-15</sup> m) to atomic radius (~10<sup>-10</sup> m) is approximately: <b>R<sub>nuc</sub> / R<sub>atom</sub> &approx; 10<sup>-5</sup> (1 : 100,000)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q19: How does the radius of a nucleus depend on its mass number A?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear radius is directly proportional to cube root of mass number: <b>R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup></b> (where R<sub>0</sub> &approx; 1.2 fm).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q20: What is the value of nuclear density for all nuclei?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear density is a constant: <b>&rho; &approx; 2.3 &times; 10<sup>17</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup></b>, completely independent of mass number A.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q21: Define mass defect of a nucleus.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Mass defect (&Delta;m) is the difference between the sum of masses of free constituent nucleons and the actual mass of the bound nucleus: <b>&Delta;m = [Z m<sub>p</sub> + (A - Z) m<sub>n</sub>] - M<sub>nucleus</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q22: What is the energy equivalent of 1 unified atomic mass unit (1 u)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1 u = 931.5 MeV</b> (or 1.494 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> Joules).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q23: Define binding energy per nucleon.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Binding energy per nucleon (E<sub>bn</sub>) is the total binding energy of a nucleus divided by its total number of nucleons A: <b>E<sub>bn</sub> = E<sub>b</sub> / A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q24: Which nucleus has the highest binding energy per nucleon?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Iron-56 (<sup>56</sup><sub>26</sub>Fe)</b> has the maximum binding energy per nucleon: <b>E<sub>bn</sub> = 8.75 MeV/nucleon</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q25: What is the range of the strong nuclear force?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The strong nuclear force is extremely short-range, acting effectively over distances of about <b>1 to 2 fm (10<sup>-15</sup> m)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q26: Is the nuclear force between two protons equal to the nuclear force between two neutrons?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Yes.</b> Strong nuclear force is strictly charge-independent: <b>F<sub>pp</sub> = F<sub>pn</sub> = F<sub>nn</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q27: Define nuclear fission.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear fission is the process in which a heavy unstable nucleus (e.g. <sup>235</sup>U) splits into two lighter nuclei of comparable masses with the release of enormous energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q28: Define nuclear fusion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear fusion is the process in which two light atomic nuclei (e.g. hydrogen isotopes) combine at extremely high temperatures to form a heavier, more stable nucleus with release of huge energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q29: What is the average energy released per fission of one Uranium-235 nucleus?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Approximately <b>200 MeV</b> (or ~0.8 MeV per nucleon).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q30: Why are extremely high temperatures (~10⁷ K) required for nuclear fusion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      High temperatures provide light nuclei with sufficient thermal kinetic energy to overcome their mutual <b>Coulomb electrostatic repulsion</b> and reach nuclear interaction range.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q31: What is the source of solar energy?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Thermonuclear Fusion</b> (primarily the Proton-Proton cycle fusing hydrogen into helium in the Sun's core).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q32: Why is total energy of an electron in an atom negative?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The negative sign indicates that the electron is <b>bound in an attractive electrostatic potential well</b> of the nucleus.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q33: What is the shortest wavelength (series limit) of the Lyman series?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      &lambda;<sub>min</sub> = 1/R = 1/(1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) &approx; <b>91.2 nm = 912 &Aring;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q34: What is the physical meaning of binding energy?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Binding energy is the minimum energy that must be supplied to a nucleus to completely separate all its constituent nucleons to infinite distances.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q35: Why do heavier nuclei have more neutrons than protons (N &gt; Z)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Extra neutrons provide additional attractive strong nuclear force without adding disruptive Coulomb electrostatic repulsion, maintaining nuclear stability.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q36: What is the value of the speed of an electron in the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      v<sub>1</sub> = c / 137 &approx; <b>2.18 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m/s</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q37: What is the orbital frequency of an electron in the n=1 orbit of hydrogen?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      f = v<sub>1</sub> / (2&pi; r<sub>1</sub>) = (2.18 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) / [2&pi; (0.529 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup>)] &approx; <b>6.6 &times; 10<sup>15</sup> Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q38: Define excitation energy of an atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The minimum energy required to elevate an electron from its ground state to a higher excited state: <b>E<sub>ex</sub> = E<sub>n</sub> - E<sub>1</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q39: Calculate the first excitation energy of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      E<sub>ex</sub> = E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub> = (-3.4 eV) - (-13.6 eV) = <b>+ 10.2 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q40: What happens to the binding energy per nucleon when a heavy nucleus undergoes fission?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The binding energy per nucleon increases from ~7.6 MeV/nucleon in <sup>235</sup>U to ~8.5 MeV/nucleon in fission fragments, releasing the difference as kinetic energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks Each &bull; Q41 to Q80)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q41: Calculate the radius of the third Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: r<sub>n</sub> = n<sup>2</sup> a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 n<sup>2</sup> &Aring;.<br>For n = 3: r<sub>3</sub> = (3)<sup>2</sup> &times; 0.529 &Aring; = 9 &times; 0.529 &Aring; = <b>4.761 &Aring; = 0.4761 nm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q42: Calculate the kinetic energy, potential energy, and total energy of an electron in the second orbit (n = 2) of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Total energy E<sub>2</sub> = -13.6 / (2)<sup>2</sup> = -13.6 / 4 = <b>- 3.4 eV</b>.<br>Kinetic Energy: <b>K = - E<sub>2</sub> = + 3.4 eV = 5.44 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J</b>.<br>Potential Energy: <b>U = 2 E<sub>2</sub> = 2 &times; (-3.4 eV) = - 6.8 eV = - 1.088 &times; 10<sup>-18</sup> J</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q43: Calculate the shortest and longest wavelengths of the Lyman series of hydrogen atom (R = 1.097 x 10⁷ m⁻¹).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Lyman series formula: 1/&lambda; = R [1/1<sup>2</sup> - 1/n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>].<br>• <b>Longest Wavelength (&lambda;<sub>max</sub>, n<sub>2</sub> = 2):</b><br>1/&lambda;<sub>max</sub> = R [1 - 1/4] = 3R/4 &rArr; &lambda;<sub>max</sub> = 4 / (3R) = 4 / [3 &times; 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>] = <b>121.5 nm = 1215 &Aring;</b>.<br>• <b>Shortest Wavelength (&lambda;<sub>min</sub>, n<sub>2</sub> = &infin;):</b><br>1/&lambda;<sub>min</sub> = R [1 - 0] = R &rArr; &lambda;<sub>min</sub> = 1 / R = 1 / (1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) = <b>91.2 nm = 912 &Aring;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q44: Find the ratio of the longest wavelength in the Lyman series to the longest wavelength in the Balmer series.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • Lyman longest (n=2 &rarr; n=1): 1/&lambda;<sub>L</sub> = R(1 - 1/4) = 3R/4 &rArr; &lambda;<sub>L</sub> = 4/(3R).<br>• Balmer longest (n=3 &rarr; n=2): 1/&lambda;<sub>B</sub> = R(1/4 - 1/9) = 5R/36 &rArr; &lambda;<sub>B</sub> = 36/(5R).<br>Ratio: &lambda;<sub>L</sub> / &lambda;<sub>B</sub> = [4/(3R)] / [36/(5R)] = (4 &times; 5) / (3 &times; 36) = 20 / 108 = <b>5 / 27 &approx; 0.185 : 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q45: The radius of a nucleus of Aluminum-27 is 3.6 fm. Calculate the radius of a nucleus of Tellurium-125.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear radius formula: R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup> &rArr; R<sub>2</sub> / R<sub>1</sub> = (A<sub>2</sub> / A<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/3</sup>.<br>R<sub>Te</sub> / R<sub>Al</sub> = (125 / 27)<sup>1/3</sup> = (5/3).<br>R<sub>Te</sub> = (5/3) &times; R<sub>Al</sub> = (5/3) &times; 3.6 fm = 5 &times; 1.2 fm = <b>6.0 fm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q46: Show that the nuclear density of an atom is independent of its mass number A.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Let a nucleus have mass number A. Total nuclear mass M &approx; A &times; m<sub>nucleon</sub>.<br>Radius R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup> &rArr; Nuclear Volume V = 4/3 &pi; R<sup>3</sup> = 4/3 &pi; (R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A.<br>Density &rho; = M / V = (A m<sub>n</sub>) / (4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A) = <b>(3 m<sub>n</sub>) / (4 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup>)</b>.<br>Since A cancels completely, &rho; depends only on constants m<sub>n</sub> and R<sub>0</sub>, proving nuclear density is <b>strictly constant for all nuclei</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q47: Calculate the mass defect and binding energy of an &alpha;-particle (Helium nucleus <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He). Given: m_p = 1.007276 u, m_n = 1.008665 u, M_He = 4.001506 u.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Constituent nucleons: Z = 2 protons, (A - Z) = 2 neutrons.<br>Total mass of free nucleons = 2 m<sub>p</sub> + 2 m<sub>n</sub> = 2(1.007276) + 2(1.008665) = 2.014552 + 2.017330 = 4.031882 u.<br>Mass Defect &Delta;m = 4.031882 u - 4.001506 u = <b>0.030376 u</b>.<br>Binding Energy E<sub>b</sub> = &Delta;m &times; 931.5 MeV = 0.030376 &times; 931.5 MeV = <b>28.295 MeV</b>.<br>Binding energy per nucleon: E<sub>bn</sub> = 28.295 / 4 = <b>7.074 MeV/nucleon</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q48: In a Geiger-Marsden experiment, calculate the distance of closest approach for a 7.7 MeV &alpha;-particle colliding head-on with a gold nucleus (Z = 79).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: K = 7.7 MeV = 7.7 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J = 1.232 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> J, Z = 79, 1/(4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup>/C<sup>2</sup>.<br>Formula: r<sub>0</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] &times; [ (2 Z e<sup>2</sup>) / K ].<br>r<sub>0</sub> = [ 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 2 &times; 79 &times; (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ] / [ 1.232 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> ] = (3.640 &times; 10<sup>-26</sup>) / (1.232 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) = <b>2.95 &times; 10<sup>-14</sup> m = 29.5 fm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q49: State two main drawbacks of Rutherford's atomic model.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Inability to explain atomic stability:</b> Classical EM theory dictates an orbiting accelerated electron must radiate energy and collapse into the nucleus in ~10<sup>-8</sup> s.<br>2. <b>Inability to explain discrete line spectra:</b> A continuously radiating electron should emit a continuous spectrum, contradicting observed sharp atomic emission lines.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q50: State the two major limitations of Bohr's model of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. It applies only to single-electron species (H, He<sup>+</sup>, Li<sup>2+</sup>) and fails for multi-electron atoms due to electron-electron interactions.<br>2. It cannot explain the fine structure of spectral lines, relative intensities of spectral transitions, or spectral splitting in magnetic (Zeeman effect) and electric (Stark effect) fields.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q51: Calculate the frequency of the photon emitted when an electron in hydrogen atom jumps from n = 4 to n = 2 orbit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Energy in n=4: E<sub>4</sub> = -13.6 / 16 = -0.85 eV.<br>Energy in n=2: E<sub>2</sub> = -13.6 / 4 = -3.40 eV.<br>&Delta;E = E<sub>4</sub> - E<sub>2</sub> = -0.85 - (-3.40) = 2.55 eV = 2.55 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J = 4.08 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J.<br>Frequency: <b>&nu; = &Delta;E / h = (4.08 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>) / (6.63 &times; 10<sup>-34</sup>) = 6.15 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q52: Distinguish between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion with three distinct points of difference.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Process:</b> Fission splits a heavy nucleus into two lighter nuclei; Fusion combines two light nuclei into a heavier nucleus.<br>2. <b>Operating Temperature:</b> Fission occurs at normal ambient temperatures; Fusion requires thermonuclear temperatures (~10<sup>7</sup> K).<br>3. <b>Energy Yield:</b> Fusion yields ~4 times more energy per unit mass than fission and produces virtually zero long-lived radioactive waste.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q53: Why is heavy water (D_2O) or graphite used as a moderator in a nuclear fission reactor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Fission of <sup>235</sup>U is efficiently triggered only by <b>slow thermal neutrons (~0.025 eV)</b>. Fast neutrons (~2 MeV) produced during fission are slowed down by elastic collisions with light nuclei in moderators (D<sub>2</sub>O, graphite) without absorbing them.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q54: What are control rods in a nuclear reactor? Name two materials used for control rods.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Control rods regulate the rate of chain reaction by absorbing excess neutrons to keep the multiplication factor k = 1 (critical state). Common materials are <b>Cadmium (Cd)</b> and <b>Boron (B)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q55: Calculate the energy released in the fusion reaction: <sup>2</sup><sub>1</sub>H + <sup>3</sup><sub>1</sub>H &rarr; <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He + <sup>1</sup><sub>0</sub>n. Given: m(<sup>2</sup>H) = 2.014102 u, m(<sup>3</sup>H) = 3.016049 u, m(<sup>4</sup>He) = 4.002603 u, m_n = 1.008665 u.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Initial mass = 2.014102 + 3.016049 = 5.030151 u.<br>Final mass = 4.002603 + 1.008665 = 5.011268 u.<br>Mass defect &Delta;m = 5.030151 - 5.011268 = 0.018883 u.<br>Energy Released: <b>Q = 0.018883 &times; 931.5 MeV = 17.59 MeV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q56: Why does binding energy per nucleon decrease for very heavy nuclei (A &gt; 170)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In heavy nuclei containing many protons, the long-range <b>Coulomb electrostatic repulsion</b> between all protons grows proportional to Z<sup>2</sup>, while short-range attractive nuclear force saturates. This net repulsive tension reduces the binding energy per nucleon.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q57: Why does binding energy per nucleon decrease for very light nuclei (A &lt; 30)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In light nuclei, a large fraction of nucleons reside on the surface and have fewer neighboring nucleons to bind with (surface effect), resulting in lower binding energy per nucleon.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q58: Explain how de-Broglie's hypothesis justifies Bohr's second postulate of angular momentum quantization.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An electron orbiting in a stable Bohr circle forms a stationary matter wave. For constructive interference, orbit circumference must equal an integral number of wavelengths: 2&pi;r = n&lambda;.<br>Substituting &lambda; = h/(mv): 2&pi;r = n[h/(mv)] &rArr; <b>m v r = n h / (2&pi;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q59: Calculate the ionization potential of singly ionized Helium atom (He+).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For hydrogen-like ion: E<sub>n</sub> = - 13.6 Z<sup>2</sup> / n<sup>2</sup> eV.<br>For He<sup>+</sup> (Z = 2, ground state n = 1): E<sub>1</sub> = -13.6 &times; (2)<sup>2</sup> / 1 = <b>- 54.4 eV</b>.<br>Ionization potential: <b>V<sub>ion</sub> = + 54.4 Volts</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q60: Find the ratio of speeds of electrons in the first orbit of Hydrogen (Z = 1) and doubly ionized Lithium Li2+ (Z = 3).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: v<sub>n</sub> &prop; Z / n. For n = 1: v &prop; Z.<br>v<sub>H</sub> / v<sub>Li</sub> = Z<sub>H</sub> / Z<sub>Li</sub> = 1 / 3 &rArr; <b>v<sub>H</sub> : v<sub>Li</sub> = 1 : 3</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q61: A radioactive sample undergoes fission releasing 200 MeV per nucleus. How many fissions per second are required to generate a power of 1 kW?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Power P = 1 kW = 1000 J/s.<br>Energy per fission E = 200 MeV = 200 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-13</sup> J = 3.2 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> J.<br>Number of fissions per second: <b>N = P / E = 1000 / (3.2 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) = 3.125 &times; 10<sup>13</sup> fissions/second</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q62: Why is an electron not found inside an atomic nucleus according to Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For an electron confined in a nucleus (&Delta;x ~ 10<sup>-14</sup> m): &Delta;p &ge; h / (4&pi;&Delta;x) &approx; 5.27 &times; 10<sup>-21</sup> kg m/s.<br>Kinetic energy E &approx; pc &approx; (5.27 &times; 10<sup>-21</sup> &times; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>)/(1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>) &approx; <b>10 MeV</b>. Because no electron in &beta;-decay has energy anywhere near ~100 MeV, electrons cannot exist as permanent residents inside nuclei.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q63: The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is -13.6 eV. What is the potential energy and kinetic energy in the first excited state (n = 2)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Total energy in n = 2: E<sub>2</sub> = -13.6 / 4 = -3.4 eV.<br>Kinetic Energy: <b>K = - E<sub>2</sub> = + 3.4 eV</b>.<br>Potential Energy: <b>U = 2 E<sub>2</sub> = - 6.8 eV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q64: State the condition for a nuclear chain reaction to be critical, subcritical, and supercritical.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Neutron multiplication factor <b>k = (Number of neutrons in current generation) / (Number in preceding generation)</b>.<br>• <b>k = 1 (Critical):</b> Steady power output (Nuclear power reactor).<br>• <b>k &lt; 1 (Subcritical):</b> Chain reaction dies out.<br>• <b>k &gt; 1 (Supercritical):</b> Chain reaction accelerates exponentially (Atomic explosion).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q65: What is the significance of the negative sign in the expression for the total energy of an electron in an orbit?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The negative sign signifies that the electron is in a <b>bound state</b> under the attractive electrostatic pull of the nucleus. Work must be done by an external agent to liberate the electron to infinity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q66: Calculate the wavelength of radiation emitted when an electron in hydrogen atom makes a transition from n = &infin; to n = 1.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1/&lambda; = R [1/1<sup>2</sup> - 1/&infin;] = R &rArr; &lambda; = 1/R = 1 / (1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>) = <b>9.116 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> m = 91.16 nm</b> (Lyman series limit).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q67: Explain why nuclear fusion is also known as a thermonuclear reaction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Nuclear fusion requires temperatures exceeding tens of millions of Kelvin (~10<sup>7</sup> K) so that the thermal kinetic energy of the nuclei is large enough to overcome their mutual electrostatic barrier. Hence it is termed a <b>thermonuclear reaction</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q68: Name two pairs of isobaric nuclei and two pairs of isotonic nuclei.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      • <b>Isobars (same A, different Z):</b> (<sup>40</sup><sub>18</sub>Ar, <sup>40</sup><sub>20</sub>Ca) and (<sup>14</sup><sub>6</sub>C, <sup>14</sup><sub>7</sub>N).<br>• <b>Isotones (same N = A - Z):</b> (<sup>14</sup><sub>6</sub>C, <sup>16</sup><sub>8</sub>O both have N = 8) and (<sup>3</sup><sub>1</sub>H, <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He both have N = 2).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q69: Calculate the ratio of the areas of the second and first Bohr orbits of hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Area A = &pi; r<sup>2</sup> &prop; r<sup>2</sup>. Since r<sub>n</sub> &prop; n<sup>2</sup>, Area A &prop; (n<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = n<sup>4</sup>.<br>A<sub>2</sub> / A<sub>1</sub> = (2/1)<sup>4</sup> = <b>16 / 1 = 16 : 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q70: What is the angular momentum of an electron in the 4th Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      L = n h / (2&pi;) = 4 &times; (6.63 &times; 10<sup>-34</sup>) / (2 &times; 3.1416) = <b>4.22 &times; 10<sup>-34</sup> J s (or kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q71: Calculate the binding energy of a deuteron (<sup>2</sup><sub>1</sub>H). Given: m_p = 1.00728 u, m_n = 1.00867 u, M_d = 2.01355 u.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Mass of constituent nucleons = 1.00728 + 1.00867 = 2.01595 u.<br>Mass defect &Delta;m = 2.01595 - 2.01355 = 0.00240 u.<br>Binding Energy: <b>E<sub>b</sub> = 0.00240 &times; 931.5 MeV = 2.236 MeV &approx; 2.24 MeV</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q72: Why cannot a single isolated neutron undergo nuclear fusion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Fusion requires strong nuclear attraction between multiple nucleons to form a bound compound nucleus. A single neutron has no partner nucleon to fuse with and decays freely via beta-decay (&tau; ~ 14.7 min).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q73: How does the orbital speed of an electron change as the principal quantum number n increases?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Orbital speed is inversely proportional to n: <b>v<sub>n</sub> &prop; 1 / n</b>. As n increases, the electron moves more slowly.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q74: How many spectral lines are emitted when an electron in hydrogen atom de-excites from n = 5 to the ground state (n = 1)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Total number of emission lines: <b>N = n(n - 1) / 2 = 5(5 - 1) / 2 = (5 &times; 4) / 2 = 10 spectral lines</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q75: State the role of a coolant in a nuclear reactor. Name two common coolants.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A coolant absorbs the immense thermal heat generated in the reactor core by fission and transfers it to a steam generator to drive electricity turbines. Examples: <b>Heavy water (D<sub>2</sub>O), Liquid Sodium (Na), or pressurized water</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q76: Why is the fusion of hydrogen into helium an exothermic reaction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The binding energy per nucleon of Helium (<sup>4</sup>He ~ 7.07 MeV/nucleon) is much greater than that of hydrogen/deuterium (~1.1 MeV/nucleon). The product nucleus is more tightly bound, releasing excess mass as energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q77: Calculate the radius of the first orbit of singly ionized helium (He+, Z = 2).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      r<sub>1</sub>(He<sup>+</sup>) = a<sub>0</sub> / Z = 0.529 &Aring; / 2 = <b>0.2645 &Aring; = 0.02645 nm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q78: What is the ratio of the frequency of revolution of an electron in n = 1 to n = 2 Bohr orbit of hydrogen?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Frequency f = v / (2&pi;r) &prop; (1/n) / (n<sup>2</sup>) = 1 / n<sup>3</sup>.<br>f<sub>1</sub> / f<sub>2</sub> = (2/1)<sup>3</sup> = <b>8 / 1 = 8 : 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q79: If the mass number of a nucleus increases by a factor of 8, by what factor does its radius increase?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      R = R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup>. When A' = 8A &rArr; R' = R<sub>0</sub> (8A)<sup>1/3</sup> = 2 R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup> = <b>2 R (doubled)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q80: Explain why nuclear forces are called 'saturated forces'.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Unlike electrostatic forces which act between all pairs of charges, a nucleon interacts only with its immediate nearest neighbors. Adding more nucleons beyond its interaction volume does not increase the binding force on that nucleon.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks Each &bull; Q81 to Q100)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q81: State Bohr's postulates for hydrogen atom. Derive expressions for the radius of the n-th orbit, velocity of electron, and total energy of electron in the n-th orbit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Bohr's Three Postulates:</b><br>\n      • <i>Stationary Orbits:</i> Electrons revolve only in non-radiating discrete circular orbits.<br>\n      • <i>Quantization:</i> Orbital angular momentum is quantized: L = m v r = n h / (2&pi;).<br>\n      • <i>Quantum Transition:</i> Radiation is emitted/absorbed during quantum jumps: h&nu; = E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub>.<br>\n      <b>2. Derivation of Orbit Radius (r<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n      Centripetal force = Electrostatic attraction:<br>\n      m v<sup>2</sup> / r = e<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; m v<sup>2</sup> r = e<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>). &nbsp;&nbsp; ...(1)<br>\n      From quantization: v = n h / (2&pi; m r). &nbsp;&nbsp; ...(2)<br>\n      Substituting (2) into (1): m [ n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> m<sup>2</sup> r<sup>2</sup>) ] r = e<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) &rArr;<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>r<sub>n</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi; m e<sup>2</sup></span></span> = n<sup>2</sup> a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 n<sup>2</sup> &Aring;</b></span>.<br>\n      <b>3. Derivation of Velocity (v<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n      Substituting r<sub>n</sub> into (2):<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>v<sub>n</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> n h</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2.18 &times; 10<sup>6</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n</span></span> m/s</b></span>.<br>\n      <b>4. Derivation of Total Energy (E<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n      • K = 1/2 m v<sup>2</sup> = e<sup>2</sup> / (8&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>n</sub>) = + m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n      • U = - e<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>n</sub>) = - m e<sup>4</sup> / (4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n      • Total Energy E<sub>n</sub> = K + U = - K:<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 18px;\"><b>E<sub>n</sub> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> n<sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">13.6</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sup>2</sup></span></span> eV</b></span>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q82: Draw the energy level diagram for hydrogen atom. Show the transitions responsible for Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, and Pfund series. Calculate the series limit for Balmer series.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Energy Levels of Hydrogen:</b><br>\n      E<sub>1</sub> = -13.6 eV, E<sub>2</sub> = -3.40 eV, E<sub>3</sub> = -1.51 eV, E<sub>4</sub> = -0.85 eV, E<sub>5</sub> = -0.54 eV, ... E<sub>&infin;</sub> = 0 eV.<br>\n      <b>2. Spectral Series Transitions:</b><br>\n      • <i>Lyman:</i> n<sub>1</sub> = 1, n<sub>2</sub> = 2, 3, 4, ... (Ultraviolet)<br>\n      • <i>Balmer:</i> n<sub>1</sub> = 2, n<sub>2</sub> = 3, 4, 5, ... (Visible)<br>\n      • <i>Paschen:</i> n<sub>1</sub> = 3, n<sub>2</sub> = 4, 5, 6, ... (Near IR)<br>\n      • <i>Brackett:</i> n<sub>1</sub> = 4, n<sub>2</sub> = 5, 6, 7, ... (IR)<br>\n      • <i>Pfund:</i> n<sub>1</sub> = 5, n<sub>2</sub> = 6, 7, 8, ... (Far IR)<br>\n      <b>3. Calculation of Series Limit for Balmer Series (n<sub>1</sub> = 2, n<sub>2</sub> = &infin;):</b><br>\n      1/&lambda; = R [ 1/2<sup>2</sup> - 1/&infin;<sup>2</sup> ] = R / 4 &rArr; &lambda;<sub>limit</sub> = 4 / R.<br>\n      &lambda;<sub>limit</sub> = 4 / (1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>) = <b>3.646 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m = 364.6 nm = 3646 &Aring;</b> (Violet limit of visible spectrum).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q83: Describe the Geiger-Marsden &alpha;-particle scattering experiment. State its observations and explain how Rutherford deduced the nuclear planetary model of the atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Experimental Setup:</b> A collimated beam of 5.5 MeV &alpha;-particles from radioactive <sup>214</sup>Bi is directed at an ultra-thin gold foil (~2.1 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m). Scintillations are recorded on a ZnS screen viewed through a rotatable microscope.<br>\n      <b>2. Key Observations:</b><br>\n      • 99.86% of particles pass straight through without deflection.<br>\n      • 0.14% scatter by &gt;1&deg;; ~1 in 8000 deflects by &gt;90&deg;; rare particles bounce back at ~180&deg;.<br>\n      <b>3. Rutherford's Nuclear Inferences:</b><br>\n      • <i>Atom is mostly empty space</i> because the vast majority of &alpha;-particles experience no deflection.<br>\n      • <i>Entire positive charge and ~99.95% of atomic mass</i> are concentrated in a tiny dense core called the <b>Nucleus</b> (radius &le; 10<sup>-14</sup> m), causing massive Coulomb repulsion on head-on &alpha;-particles.<br>\n      • <i>Electrons revolve around the nucleus</i> in planetary orbits, balancing electrostatic attraction with centripetal force.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q84: Draw the binding energy per nucleon (E_bn) curve as a function of mass number A. Explain its main features and how it explains both nuclear fission and nuclear fusion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Features of the E<sub>bn</sub> vs A Curve:</b><br>\n      • <i>Peak:</i> Maximum at <sup>56</sup>Fe (E<sub>bn</sub> = 8.75 MeV/nucleon).<br>\n      • <i>Plateau:</i> Nearly constant ~8.5 MeV/nucleon for intermediate nuclei (30 &le; A &le; 170) due to short-range saturation of nuclear forces.<br>\n      • <i>Heavy Nuclei Droop:</i> Drops to ~7.6 MeV/nucleon for A &gt; 170 (e.g. <sup>235</sup>U) due to Coulomb proton repulsion.<br>\n      • <i>Light Nuclei Droop:</i> Drops sharply for A &lt; 30 (e.g. <sup>2</sup>H ~ 1.1 MeV/nucleon) due to surface nucleon effects.<br>\n      <b>2. Explanation of Nuclear Fission:</b> When a heavy nucleus (<sup>235</sup>U, E<sub>bn</sub> ~ 7.6 MeV) splits into two medium nuclei (A ~ 100, E<sub>bn</sub> ~ 8.5 MeV), binding energy per nucleon increases by ~0.9 MeV/nucleon, releasing 235 &times; 0.9 &approx; <b>200 MeV of energy</b>.<br>\n      <b>3. Explanation of Nuclear Fusion:</b> When light nuclei (<sup>2</sup>H, E<sub>bn</sub> ~ 1.1 MeV) fuse into <sup>4</sup>He (E<sub>bn</sub> ~ 7.07 MeV), binding energy per nucleon increases by ~6 MeV/nucleon, releasing colossal energy per unit mass.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q85: Derive the expression for the distance of closest approach of an &alpha;-particle of energy K colliding head-on with a nucleus of atomic number Z. Calculate its value for 5.5 MeV &alpha;-particles on gold (Z = 79).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Derivation:</b><br>\n      In a head-on collision (impact parameter b = 0), at closest approach r<sub>0</sub>, the entire initial kinetic energy K of the &alpha;-particle (q<sub>1</sub> = +2e) is converted into electrostatic potential energy of interaction with the gold nucleus (q<sub>2</sub> = +Ze):<br>\n      K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(2e)(Ze)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 Z e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sub>0</sub></span></span>.<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>r<sub>0</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 Z e<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> K</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n      <b>2. Calculation for Gold (Z = 79, K = 5.5 MeV):</b><br>\n      K = 5.5 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> J = 8.8 &times; 10<sup>-13</sup> J.<br>\n      r<sub>0</sub> = [ 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 2 &times; 79 &times; (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ] / [ 8.8 &times; 10<sup>-13</sup> ] = (3.640 &times; 10<sup>-26</sup>) / (8.8 &times; 10<sup>-13</sup>) = <b>4.13 &times; 10<sup>-14</sup> m = 41.3 fm</b>.<br>\n      This establishes an upper limit on the radius of the gold nucleus (R &lt; 41.3 fm).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q86: Explain the working of a nuclear fission reactor with a labeled block schematic. Describe the functions of: (i) Nuclear Fuel, (ii) Moderator, (iii) Control Rods, (iv) Coolant, (v) Radiation Shielding.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Functions of Essential Components of Nuclear Reactor:</b><br>\n      • <b>(i) Nuclear Fuel:</b> Fissionable material, typically enriched Uranium (2-4% <sup>235</sup>U in <sup>238</sup>U) or Plutonium-239, fabricated into cylindrical fuel rods sealed in zirconium cladding.<br>\n      • <b>(ii) Moderator:</b> Slows down fast fission neutrons (2 MeV) to thermal energy (0.025 eV) via elastic collisions with light atoms without capturing them (e.g. Heavy water D<sub>2</sub>O, pure graphite).<br>\n      • <b>(iii) Control Rods:</b> Made of strong neutron absorbers like Cadmium (Cd) or Boron (B) inserted into the core to maintain a steady critical multiplication factor k = 1.<br>\n      • <b>(iv) Coolant:</b> Fluid (pressurized water, heavy water, or molten liquid sodium) circulating through the core to extract fission thermal heat and generate steam for electricity turbines.<br>\n      • <b>(v) Radiation Shielding:</b> Thick 2-meter concrete and lead biological shield surrounding the reactor vessel to absorb hazardous gamma radiation and escaped neutrons.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q87: Explain the Proton-Proton cycle of stellar nuclear fusion occurring in the Sun. Calculate the net Q-value of the reaction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. The Three Steps of the Proton-Proton (P-P) Cycle:</b><br>\n      • Step 1: <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H + <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H &rarr; <sup>2</sup><sub>1</sub>H + e<sup>+</sup> + &nu; + 0.42 MeV (occurs twice)<br>\n      • Step 2: e<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>-</sup> &rarr; 2&gamma; + 1.02 MeV (annihilation, occurs twice)<br>\n      • Step 3: <sup>2</sup><sub>1</sub>H + <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H &rarr; <sup>3</sup><sub>2</sub>He + &gamma; + 5.49 MeV (occurs twice)<br>\n      • Step 4: <sup>3</sup><sub>2</sub>He + <sup>3</sup><sub>2</sub>He &rarr; <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He + 2 <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H + 12.86 MeV<br>\n      <b>2. Net Overall Reaction:</b><br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>4 <sup>1</sup><sub>1</sub>H + 2 e<sup>-</sup> &rarr; <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He + 2 &nu; + 6 &gamma; + Q</b></span>.<br>\n      <b>3. Net Energy Released:</b><br>\n      Q = 2(0.42) + 2(1.02) + 2(5.49) + 12.86 = <b>26.7 MeV</b>.<br>\n      Four hydrogen protons fuse into one helium nucleus, releasing 26.7 MeV of thermonuclear energy!\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q88: Using Bohr's postulates, derive the Rydberg-Ritz formula for the wavelength of spectral lines emitted by a hydrogen atom.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Energy of Orbit Levels:</b><br>\n      From Bohr's theory, energy of n-th orbit is: E<sub>n</sub> = - [ m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup>) ] (1 / n<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n      <b>2. Bohr's Third Postulate (Quantum Jump from n<sub>2</sub> to n<sub>1</sub>):</b><br>\n      &Delta;E = E<sub>2</sub> - E<sub>1</sub> = h &nu; = h (c / &lambda;).<br>\n      h c / &lambda; = [ - m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>) ] - [ - m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup> n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>) ].<br>\n      h c / &lambda; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> h<sup>2</sup></span></span> [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> ].<br>\n      <b>3. Dividing by hc:</b><br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17.5px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&lambda;</span></span> = &nu;&#772; = [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m e<sup>4</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c h<sup>3</sup></span></span> ] [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> ] = R [ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> ]</b></span>,<br>\n      where <b>R = m e<sup>4</sup> / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c h<sup>3</sup>) = 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m<sup>-1</sup></b> is the Rydberg Constant.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q89: Find the wavelength of the photon emitted when an electron in hydrogen atom jumps from the fourth excited state to the ground state. Identify the spectral series and calculate its frequency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      Fourth excited state corresponds to <b>n<sub>2</sub> = 5</b>; Ground state is <b>n<sub>1</sub> = 1</b>.<br>\n      Since n<sub>1</sub> = 1, this line belongs to the <b>Lyman Series (Ultraviolet region)</b>.<br>\n      <b>1. Wavelength Calculation:</b><br>\n      1/&lambda; = R [ 1/1<sup>2</sup> - 1/5<sup>2</sup> ] = R [ 1 - 1/25 ] = 24R / 25.<br>\n      &lambda; = 25 / (24 R) = 25 / (24 &times; 1.097 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) = 25 / (2.6328 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>9.495 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> m = 94.95 nm = 949.5 &Aring;</b>.<br>\n      <b>2. Frequency Calculation:</b><br>\n      &nu; = c / &lambda; = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (9.495 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup>) = <b>3.16 &times; 10<sup>15</sup> Hz</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q90: Explain why nuclear density is independent of mass number A. If the mass of an atom is 56 u and radius is 4.6 fm, calculate its nuclear density.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Theoretical Independence of &rho; on A:</b><br>\n      Nuclear mass M = A &times; m<sub>u</sub> (m<sub>u</sub> &approx; 1.66 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup> kg).<br>\n      Nuclear Volume V = 4/3 &pi; R<sup>3</sup> = 4/3 &pi; (R<sub>0</sub> A<sup>1/3</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A.<br>\n      &rho; = M / V = (A m<sub>u</sub>) / (4/3 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> A) = <b>(3 m<sub>u</sub>) / (4 &pi; R<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup>) = const</b>.<br>\n      <b>2. Numerical Calculation for Iron-56 (M = 56 u, R = 4.6 fm):</b><br>\n      M = 56 &times; 1.66 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup> kg = 9.296 &times; 10<sup>-26</sup> kg.<br>\n      V = 4/3 &times; 3.1416 &times; (4.6 &times; 10<sup>-15</sup> m)<sup>3</sup> = 4.1888 &times; (9.7336 &times; 10<sup>-44</sup>) = 4.077 &times; 10<sup>-43</sup> m<sup>3</sup>.<br>\n      &rho; = (9.296 &times; 10<sup>-26</sup>) / (4.077 &times; 10<sup>-43</sup>) = <b>2.28 &times; 10<sup>17</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup></b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q91: Compare the properties of gravitational force, electrostatic force, and strong nuclear force across five fundamental criteria.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>Comprehensive Fundamental Forces Comparison:</b><br>\n      • <i>1. Relative Strength:</i> Gravitational (1) : Electrostatic (10<sup>36</sup>) : Strong Nuclear (10<sup>38</sup>). Nuclear force is ~100&times; stronger than EM force.<br>\n      • <i>2. Range:</i> Gravitational (Infinite, 1/r<sup>2</sup>); Electrostatic (Infinite, 1/r<sup>2</sup>); Nuclear (Extremely short-range ~1 - 2 fm, vanishes beyond 2 fm).<br>\n      • <i>3. Nature:</i> Gravitational (Always attractive); Electrostatic (Attractive or repulsive); Nuclear (Strongly attractive at 1-2 fm, repulsive at r &lt; 0.8 fm).<br>\n      • <i>4. Charge Dependence:</i> Gravitational (Mass-dependent); Electrostatic (Charge-dependent); Nuclear (Strictly charge-independent F<sub>pp</sub> = F<sub>pn</sub> = F<sub>nn</sub>).<br>\n      • <i>5. Origin / Mediator:</i> Gravitational (Graviton); Electrostatic (Photon); Nuclear (Mesons / Gluons).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q92: Calculate the Q-value and kinetic energy of emitted &alpha;-particle in the decay of Radium: <sup>226</sup><sub>88</sub>Ra &rarr; <sup>222</sup><sub>86</sub>Rn + <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He. Given: M(Ra) = 226.02540 u, M(Rn) = 222.01757 u, M(He) = 4.00260 u.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Mass Defect Calculation:</b><br>\n      Mass of Parent = 226.02540 u.<br>\n      Mass of Daughters = 222.01757 + 4.00260 = 226.02017 u.<br>\n      &Delta;m = 226.02540 - 226.02017 = 0.00523 u.<br>\n      <b>2. Total Q-Value:</b><br>\n      <b>Q = 0.00523 &times; 931.5 MeV = 4.872 MeV</b>.<br>\n      <b>3. Kinetic Energy of &alpha;-Particle (Conservation of Momentum):</b><br>\n      K<sub>&alpha;</sub> = Q &times; [ (A - 4) / A ] = 4.872 &times; (222 / 226) = <b>4.786 MeV</b>.<br>\n      (Recoil kinetic energy of Radon daughter K<sub>Rn</sub> = 4.872 - 4.786 = 0.086 MeV).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q93: Explain how the wave nature of electrons accounts for the stability of non-radiating Bohr stationary orbits.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Standing Matter Waves in Circular Orbits:</b><br>\n      According to Louis de-Broglie, an electron orbiting the nucleus is accompanied by a matter wave of wavelength &lambda; = h/(mv).<br>\n      • For an orbit to be non-radiating and stationary, the matter wave must interfere constructively with itself upon completing each revolution, forming a <b>continuous standing wave pattern</b>.<br>\n      • If the wave were out of phase, destructive interference would rapidly extinguish the wave amplitude and electron orbit.<br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Proof:</b><br>\n      Constructive standing wave condition: Circumference = Integral number of wavelengths:<br>\n      2 &pi; r<sub>n</sub> = n &lambda; &nbsp;&nbsp; (n = 1, 2, 3, ...).<br>\n      Substituting de-Broglie formula &lambda; = h / (m v<sub>n</sub>):<br>\n      2 &pi; r<sub>n</sub> = n [ h / (m v<sub>n</sub>) ] &rArr; <b>m v<sub>n</sub> r<sub>n</sub> = n h / (2&pi;) = n &hbar;</b>.<br>\n      Thus, Bohr's empirical quantization rule is rigorously derived from quantum wave mechanics!\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q94: Explain the term 'Mass Defect' and 'Binding Energy'. Calculate the binding energy per nucleon of Nitrogen-14 (<sup>14</sup><sub>7</sub>N). Given: m_p = 1.007825 u, m_n = 1.008665 u, M(<sup>14</sup>N) = 14.003074 u.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Definitions:</b><br>\n      • <i>Mass Defect (&Delta;m):</i> The difference between the mass of free constituent nucleons and the actual mass of the bound nucleus: &Delta;m = (Z m<sub>p</sub> + N m<sub>n</sub>) - M.<br>\n      • <i>Binding Energy (E<sub>b</sub>):</i> Energy released when free nucleons bind together to form a nucleus: E<sub>b</sub> = &Delta;m &times; 931.5 MeV.<br>\n      <b>2. Calculation for Nitrogen-14 (Z = 7, N = 7):</b><br>\n      Mass of free nucleons = 7(1.007825) + 7(1.008665) = 7.054775 + 7.060655 = 14.115430 u.<br>\n      Mass defect &Delta;m = 14.115430 - 14.003074 = <b>0.112356 u</b>.<br>\n      Total Binding Energy E<sub>b</sub> = 0.112356 &times; 931.5 MeV = <b>104.66 MeV</b>.<br>\n      Binding Energy per Nucleon: <b>E<sub>bn</sub> = E<sub>b</sub> / 14 = 104.66 / 14 = 7.476 MeV/nucleon</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q95: A 1000 MW nuclear power plant operates at 33% thermal efficiency. Calculate the mass of Uranium-235 consumed per year assuming 200 MeV energy released per fission.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Total Thermal Power Required:</b><br>\n      P<sub>electrical</sub> = 1000 MW = 10<sup>9</sup> W. Efficiency &eta; = 0.33.<br>\n      P<sub>thermal</sub> = P<sub>electrical</sub> / &eta; = 10<sup>9</sup> / 0.33 = 3.03 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> W.<br>\n      <b>2. Total Thermal Energy Generated in 1 Year (365 days):</b><br>\n      E<sub>total</sub> = (3.03 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> W) &times; (365 &times; 24 &times; 3600 s) = 9.556 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> Joules.<br>\n      <b>3. Energy per Fission:</b><br>\n      E<sub>fission</sub> = 200 MeV = 200 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-13</sup> J = 3.2 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> J.<br>\n      Total fissions per year N = E<sub>total</sub> / E<sub>fission</sub> = (9.556 &times; 10<sup>16</sup>) / (3.2 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) = 2.986 &times; 10<sup>27</sup> fissions.<br>\n      <b>4. Mass of <sup>235</sup>U Consumed:</b><br>\n      Moles of U = N / N<sub>A</sub> = (2.986 &times; 10<sup>27</sup>) / (6.022 &times; 10<sup>23</sup>) = 4958.5 moles.<br>\n      <b>Mass = 4958.5 &times; 0.235 kg = 1165 kg &approx; 1.165 metric tons of <sup>235</sup>U per year</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q96: State the main assumptions and success of Bohr's atomic model. Why is the angular momentum of an electron in a hydrogen atom quantized?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Key Assumptions:</b><br>\n      • Electrons revolve in stable non-radiating orbits.<br>\n      • Angular momentum is quantized: L = n&hbar;.<br>\n      • Emission/absorption occurs only during discrete quantum transitions: &Delta;E = h&nu;.<br>\n      <b>2. Major Successes:</b><br>\n      • Successfully calculated the dimensions of atoms (a<sub>0</sub> = 0.529 &Aring;) and ionization energy (13.6 eV).<br>\n      • Provided the first theoretical derivation of the Rydberg constant and explained all hydrogen spectral series.<br>\n      <b>3. Origin of Quantization:</b><br>\n      Quantization arises because the electron behaves as a de-Broglie wave. An orbit is stable only if the circumference fits an integral number of whole wavelengths (2&pi;r = n&lambda;), which naturally quantizes angular momentum L = nh/(2&pi;).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q97: Explain the term 'Impact Parameter' in &alpha;-particle scattering. Deduce how the scattering angle &theta; varies as the impact parameter b decreases from infinity to zero.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Definition:</b> Impact parameter (b) is the perpendicular distance of the initial velocity vector of the &alpha;-particle from the center of the target nucleus.<br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Relation:</b><br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16.5px;\"><b>b = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Z e<sup>2</sup> cot(&theta;/2)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> K</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n      <b>3. Variation of Scattering Angle &theta; with b:</b><br>\n      • <i>Large Impact Parameter (b &rarr; &infin;):</i> &alpha;-particle passes far from the nucleus, experiencing negligible Coulomb force &rarr; Scattering angle <b>&theta; &approx; 0&deg; (no deflection)</b>.<br>\n      • <i>Intermediate Impact Parameter (b ~ 10-100 fm):</i> Experiences significant repulsive force &rarr; Deflects by moderate angles (0&deg; &lt; &theta; &lt; 90&deg;).<br>\n      • <i>Zero Impact Parameter (b = 0, Head-on collision):</i> cot(&theta;/2) = 0 &rArr; &theta;/2 = 90&deg; &rArr; <b>&theta; = 180&deg; (complete backward rebound)</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q98: Calculate the ionization energy and binding energy of an electron in the ground state of a Hydrogen atom and a Singly Ionized Helium atom (He+).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      Formula: E<sub>n</sub> = - 13.6 Z<sup>2</sup> / n<sup>2</sup> eV.<br>\n      <b>1. Hydrogen Atom (Z = 1, n = 1):</b><br>\n      • Ground State Energy: E<sub>1</sub> = -13.6 (1)<sup>2</sup> / 1 = <b>- 13.6 eV</b>.<br>\n      • Binding Energy: <b>13.6 eV</b>.<br>\n      • Ionization Energy: E<sub>&infin;</sub> - E<sub>1</sub> = 0 - (-13.6) = <b>+ 13.6 eV</b>.<br>\n      <b>2. Singly Ionized Helium He<sup>+</sup> (Z = 2, n = 1):</b><br>\n      • Ground State Energy: E<sub>1</sub> = -13.6 (2)<sup>2</sup> / 1 = -13.6 &times; 4 = <b>- 54.4 eV</b>.<br>\n      • Binding Energy: <b>54.4 eV</b>.<br>\n      • Ionization Energy: 0 - (-54.4) = <b>+ 54.4 eV</b>.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q99: Why is nuclear fusion considered a cleaner and safer source of energy compared to nuclear fission?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Advantages of Fusion over Fission:</b><br>\n      • <b>Zero High-Level Radioactive Waste:</b> Fusion products (primarily Helium-4) are non-toxic, non-radioactive, and chemically inert, unlike long-lived hazardous fission waste (strontium, cesium, plutonium) requiring thousands of years of geological storage.<br>\n      • <b>Abundant Fuel Supply:</b> Deuterium is extracted easily from ocean water, and tritium is bred from abundant lithium. Ocean water contains enough deuterium to power humanity for millions of years.<br>\n      • <b>No Risk of Meltdown:</b> Fusion requires precise plasma confinement. Any disturbance immediately cools the plasma and halts the reaction instantly (inherently fail-safe).<br>\n      • <b>Zero Proliferation:</b> Fusion reactors do not produce weapons-grade fissile isotopes.\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q100: Explain the term 'Critical Mass' in nuclear chain reactions. What happens if the mass of fissionable material is: (i) less than critical mass, (ii) equal to critical mass, (iii) greater than critical mass?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      \n      <b>1. Definition of Critical Mass:</b> The minimum mass of fissionable material (e.g. <sup>235</sup>U or <sup>239</sup>Pu) required to sustain a self-perpetuating nuclear chain reaction.<br>\n      <b>2. Three Mass Regimes:</b><br>\n      • <b>(i) Subcritical Mass (M &lt; M<sub>crit</sub>):</b> The surface-area-to-volume ratio is too large; more neutrons leak out through the surface than are produced by fission (k &lt; 1). The chain reaction rapidly dies out.<br>\n      • <b>(ii) Critical Mass (M = M<sub>crit</sub>):</b> Neutron production rate exactly balances neutron loss (k = 1). Chain reaction proceeds at a steady, controlled rate (used in commercial nuclear power reactors).<br>\n      • <b>(iii) Supercritical Mass (M &gt; M<sub>crit</sub>):</b> Fission neutron production far exceeds loss (k &gt; 1). The reaction rate escalates exponentially in microseconds, releasing catastrophic explosive energy (atomic bomb).\n    \n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

